doc:    "A match arm whose body does not fit beside its `=>` gets a block, and the guard above it was broken to make room — `() if n\\n    > 0 => {`. Formatting that again put the guard back on one line, because the body is a block by then and the arm head fits, so `buri format` and `buri format --check` disagreed about the file forever. Found by mutating cli/tests/formatting/match_a_guard_and_an_or_pattern/input.buri. It is older than the fixes in this corpus and was hidden by them: a fixed-point finding has no `error:` line, so its signature is the headline alone, and while the import-path case was OPEN every other fixed-point finding deduped into it and was skipped. Fixing that one is what made this one visible. The cause is a placement: `match_expr` put the arm head in front of the `Alt` that chooses between `=> body` and `=> { body }`, and a guard is a group, so it was measured against whatever the printer had stacked behind it — where `fits` reads an `Alt` as its *first* candidate, the unbraced body that was about to be rejected. Fixed by putting the head inside both candidates, so the guard is laid out only once the body's shape is settled, which is the question the second run asks. The printed shape is pinned by cli/tests/formatting/match_a_wrapped_guard_keeps_its_head_whole/."
oracle: "roundtrip"
status: FIXED
